- `.prettierrc` uses `prettier-plugin-tailwindcss` for class-order enforcement, and ESLint uses `eslint-config-next`. Both are lint/format-enforced, so class ordering/formatting is out of scope per your instructions (already runs clean).

**(a) Documented-standard violations: none.**
There is no documented styling/layout convention in this repo that a one-line Tailwind utility swap (`items-center` → `items-stretch`) could violate. `AGENTS.md`'s only relevant note (Next.js vendored-docs rule) doesn't apply to a class-name change.

**(b) Baseline smell judgement calls:**

Given the diff is a single class token changed on one line, almost none of the Fowler ch.3 smells have enough surface area to apply. Specifically:

- No Duplicated Code, Feature Envy, Data Clumps, Primitive Obsession, Repeated Switches, Shotgun Surgery, Divergent Change, Speculative Generality, Message Chains, Middle Man, or Refused Bequest — none of these are structural concepts this hunk touches.
- **Mysterious Name** — arguably not applicable; `items-stretch` is a standard, self-documenting Tailwind utility, not a custom identifier.

The only soft observation worth flagging as a judgement call (not a named Fowler smell, but adjacent to "Divergent Change" in spirit): the fix changes cross-axis alignment for the *entire* wrapping `<div>` (line `mt-24 flex flex-col items-stretch justify-center`), which also wraps the `<Cards>` grid and nothing else currently — so today there's no risk of unintended stretching of sibling content. But if a future sibling is added inside this same wrapper expecting `items-center` behavior (e.g., a centered caption or button below the cards), it will silently inherit `items-stretch` too. That's a latent coupling to watch, not a bug now — worth a one-line comment or scoping the class to `<Cards>` itself if the wrapper is likely to gain siblings.

**Bottom line:** clean, minimal, correctly-scoped fix; no hard violations; only a minor "watch for future siblings" judgement call.
